=== MINILANG REVERSE-ENGINEERING REPORT ===

1. ARCHITECTURE: Toy compiler pipeline (Source -> Lexer -> Parser -> Compiler -> VM -> Output). Only Lexer and Parser fully implemented. Compiler.java and VM.java are MISSING. Main.java only runs lexer, not full pipeline.

2. FILE-BY-FILE:
- Main.java: Incomplete entry point (only tokenizes, ignores args flags)
- lexer/*: Complete (Lexer, Token, TokenType)
- parser/*: Complete (Parser, Expr, Stmt)
- compiler/*: Partial (Opcode, Instruction, Bytecode exist; Compiler.java MISSING)
- vm/*: Partial (Value, CallFrame, RuntimeError exist; VM.java MISSING)
- pom.xml: Maven build, JUnit test dependency, shade plugin
- README.md: Documents full pipeline that doesn't exist in code

3. RUNTIME FLOW: Source file -> Lexer.tokenize() -> List<Token> -> Main prints tokens. Parser/Compiler/VM never invoked.

4. API FLOW: CLI only: java minilang.Main <file.ml>. Debug flags (--tokens, --ast, --bytecode) unimplemented.

5. DATA FLOW: Source string -> Tokens -> (intended) AST -> Bytecode -> Execution.

6. FRONTEND/BACKEND: None. CLI-only educational tool.

7. DEPENDENCIES: Java 21+, Maven, JUnit Jupiter 5.10.2 (test), Maven Shade Plugin.

8. AUTH/SECURITY: None. No sandbox, no validation, direct file reads.

9. BUILD/DEPLOY: mvn compile / mvn package. Main class: minilang.Main.

10. DEAD/SUSPICIOUS: README overstates capabilities. f1.ml contains inappropriate content. Lexer assigns EQUAL to both = and ==.

11. BUGS/DEBT:
- Main.java doesn't use Parser/Compiler/VM
- Compiler.java MISSING
- VM.java MISSING
- No tests in src/test/
- Parser has potential ClassCastException
- No error recovery

12. FILES TO UNDERSTAND BEFORE MODIFYING:
Main.java, Lexer.java, TokenType.java, Parser.java, Expr.java, Stmt.java, Opcode.java

13. EXPLANATION FOR NEW TEAM MEMBER:
MiniLang is an educational toy language inspired by Crafting Interpreters. The lexer and parser are complete and working. The compiler and VM do not exist yet — you need to implement them to make the language execute. Main.java currently only prints tokens. Start by reading Main.java, then Lexer, Parser, and the compiler/VM structures.
